    "phase": "report",
    "reply": "{\n  \"claim\": \"The recovery kernel's rewrite-instantiation checks are inconsistent with the documented recover_json_object rule: several rewrites that should be valid additions after a fenced object are reported as INVALID_INSTANTIATION or moved, even though the documented rule says the last object inside a code fence is scored and later top-level objects are candidates.\",\n  \"expected\": \"Adding a top-level JSON object after a fenced object should be a valid instantiation and should not change the recovered object when the last fenced object remains the same; the recovered object should remain the fenced object.\",\n  \"observed\": \"Multiple cases where a new object is appended after a fence are marked INVALID_INSTANTIATION with detail 'the rewritten text is no cell of the grammar', and some cases where the appended object is outside the fence are marked moved, changing the recovered object from the fenced object to the appended object.\",\n  \"reproducer\": \"```\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}\",\n  \"source_basis\": \"recover_json_object: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' and 'Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate'.\",\n  \"execution_evidence\": \"The provided run results show INVALID_INSTANTIATION for inputs such as '```\\\\n{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\": 1}\\\\n```\\\\n{\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\": 2}' with rewrite 'add', and moved for '```\\\\n{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\": 1}\\\\nHere is the result:\\\\n```\\\\n{\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\": 2}' with rewrite 'add'.\",\n  \"scope\": \"All recovery-kernel rewrite instantiations that append a top-level JSON object after a fenced object.\",\n  \"uncertainty\": \"The grammar for cells is not fully specified in the provided rules, so it is possible the INVALID_INSTANTIATION results are due to a separate cell-grammar constraint not shown here; however, the moved results directly contradict the documented scoring rule for fenced objects.\"\n}"
